      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Diagnostic of chest pain in the emergency services supposes a serious problem. This study wants objective if the new non enzymatic markers of myocardial necrosis (T troponin and I troponin) may be an aid for the diagnostic and prognostic stratification of patients with myocardial ischemia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
          "content": "82 successive patients who went to the emergency room for chest pain were studied. Electrocardiogram and blood samples were obtained at 0,4, and 12 house of admission. A clinic-evolutive study was performed at discharge, and they were classified in 3 categories: a) myocardial infarction, b) unstable angina (Braunwald classification) and no coronary pains."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In the 27 patients with myocardial infarction the markers of troponin group were more sensitive than creatinine kinase to determine myocardial necrosis at 4 hours of admission. Of 41 patients with unstable angina, 34% were of Braunwald III-B class. Troponin group markers discriminate a group of high risk patients, 70% of the patients that need an emergency coronarography for bad clinical evolution were troponin group marker positive."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The use in the emergency room of troponin necrosis markers (T and I troponin) allows to optimize the sanitary resources, detecting quickly the patients with acute myocardial infarction and discriminate a group of high risk of patients with angina."
        }
      ]},
